{"step_index":196,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-30T21:59:41Z","thinking":"Examining the specified code section to understand the current implementation of the checkout workflow initialization. The initial focus is on how the close button and backdrop are handled, specifically the event listener associated with the close button. Further investigation is needed into the modal's reset logic and any associated dialogs that may require updates.\n\n\nInvestigating the conditional logic tied to the delivery mode switcher, focusing on how different UI elements are shown or hidden based on the selected mode. The code appears to manage the visibility of address input fields and map elements, likely controlling the user's data entry based on their delivery preference (delivery vs. other options). This implementation suggests a dynamic UI update based on user interaction with the delivery mode selection.\n\n\nThe delivery mode switcher's logic dictates UI element visibility. Elements associated with location input or map display are hidden when the mode is not 'delivery'. Conversely, a specific element is shown when the delivery mode is set to 'dinein'. It's essential to ensure UI responsiveness. The geolocation trigger attempts to retrieve the user's location via the browser's geolocation API. If the browser lacks this feature, an error message is displayed.\n\n\nGPS location retrieval uses the browser's geolocation API, updating customer location data upon success and showing an error message on failure. Payment method selection dynamically highlights the chosen payment option by updating CSS classes. Input validation removes error indicators on user input for various form fields.\n\nFurther analysis reveals the \"Done\" button in the WhatsApp dialog resets the checkout modal, clears the cart, and resets form and button states. Form submission is handled, targeting the `handleOrder` function after a conditional check.\n\n\nIn